<TopicPage slug="subjects/development-studies/foundational/what-is-development">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Development studies begins with a definition fight: <em>what does it mean for a society to
    develop?</em> Money is the easiest answer and the wrongest complete one. A country's income
    is the easiest thing to count — one number per nation per year — and counting it has real
    uses. But the twentieth century filled the record with countries that grew rich on paper
    while their people died young (oil economies with health systems to match their
    refineries), and others that achieved long, literate, healthy lives on modest incomes
    (Costa Rica, Kerala, Sri Lanka — the standard citations). Whatever development is, it is
    not fully captured by GDP. The modern synthesis — Amartya Sen's <strong>capabilities
    approach</strong>, articulated in <em>Development as Freedom</em> (1999) — defines
    development as the expansion of what people can <em>be and do</em>: to be nourished and
    educated, to live long, to participate in public life, to speak without fear. Income
    matters because it buys capabilities; it is a means whose conversion into outcomes varies
    enormously.
  </p>
  <p>
    That definition fight is not academic etiquette; it decides policy. Choose GDP growth as
    the measure and you get a development project of investment, exports and liberalisation;
    choose the poverty headcount and you get transfers and targeting; choose school completion
    and vaccination and you get service delivery; choose capabilities and you get a project
    that also asks about rights, gender and voice. Measurement, in this field, is not
    bookkeeping — it is the argument. This page works through the concepts, the measurement
    toolkit, and the distribution problem that averages create.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="measuring more than money" widget="equationStep" config={devCfg} />

  <h2>Development concepts: growth, modernisation, dependency, capabilities</h2>
  <p>
    The field's first distinction is <strong>growth versus development</strong>: growth is
    more output (GDP rising); development is a transformation in how people live. The
    distinction's history is the field's intellectual history. <strong>Modernisation
    theory</strong> of the 1950s–60s (Walt Rostow's <em>stages of economic growth</em> —
    traditional society, preconditions, take-off, drive to maturity, age of high mass
    consumption) read development as a single track all nations would climb with investment
    and technology. <strong>Dependency theory</strong> answered from the global South:
    the world economy was structured so that peripheries exported raw materials and imported
    manufactures, and the terms of trade themselves drained development — underdevelopment
    was made, not missed. The debate matured rather than resolved: we now ask which
    institutions (states capable of both steering and restraint), which industrial strategies
    (export discipline worked in East Asia), which histories (colonial extraction legacies)
    and which geographies separate convergers from non-convergers — and each unit of this
    subject takes one of those answers seriously.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>capabilities approach</strong> reframed the ends: Sen's question is not "how
    much income?" but "what can this person actually do and be?" — and the same income
    converts differently depending on health, education, gender norms, disability, and public
    goods. A wheelchair user in a city without pavements has income but not mobility; a woman
    barred from work has purchasing power but not livelihood. Martha Nussbaum's list of central
    capabilities made the philosophy operational; the UN's adoption of the approach made it
    institutional. <strong>Sustainable development</strong> added the time dimension — the
    Brundtland Commission's 1987 formulation: meeting present needs without compromising future
    generations' ability to meet theirs — and the policy architecture followed: the Millennium
    Development Goals (2000, eight goals) and the Sustainable Development Goals (2015, seventeen
    goals and 169 targets) are capabilities thinking written as a checklist the whole world
    reports against.
  </p>

  <h2>Measuring development: GDP, HDI and beyond</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>GDP</strong> — the market value of all final goods and services produced in a
    period — earns its centrality by being countable and comparable, but every word of the
    definition is a limitation. <em>Market value</em>: unpaid care work, household labour and
    the informal economy largely vanish (which is a large share of activity in poor countries,
    and systematically the work of women). <em>Produced</em>: depletion of forests, aquifers
    and stability counts as income, not cost — a country can log a booming GDP while liquidating
    its natural capital. <em>Per capita</em> (when we divide): the mean knows nothing about the
    distribution beneath it. And cross-country comparison demands <strong>purchasing power
    parity</strong> (PPP) conversion, because a dollar buys far more haircuts and rice in Lagos
    than in London — PPP adjustments are why poor countries look substantially less poor on
    the honest charts than on market-exchange-rate ones.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="income and life expectancy — the Preston curve" widget="plotter" config={hdiCfg} />
  <p>
    The <strong>Human Development Index</strong> (UNDP, 1990) is the field's flagship
    correction: a single number bundling three dimensions — a long life (life expectancy at
    birth), knowledge (expected and mean years of schooling) and a decent standard of living
    (GNI per capita, PPP) — combined as a geometric mean. It is deliberately crude, and its
    value is diagnostic: countries with similar incomes land at very different HDIs, and the
    oil states with GDP-to-match-Europe but development-to-match-much-poorer-countries are the
    standing counter-example the index was built to catch. The plot above shows the
    <strong>Preston curve</strong> (Samuel Preston, 1975): life expectancy rises steeply with
    income and then flattens — the first dollars per capita buy vaccines, sanitation, nutrition
    and maternal survival; the next forty thousand buy less life. The scatter around the curve
    is the interesting part: countries far <em>above</em> it (strong public health on modest
    incomes) and far <em>below</em> it (weak institutions on rich ones) prove that income
    converts into health through policy, not automatically. Development policy, the widget
    says, is largely the study of moving a country <em>up</em> its curve, not just along it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Beyond the HDI</strong>, the toolkit multiplies by question. The <strong>Multidimensional
    Poverty Index</strong> (UNDP and OPHI, 2010) counts overlapping deprivations — nutrition,
    child mortality, schooling years, cooking fuel, sanitation, water, electricity, flooring,
    assets — and finds roughly a billion people multidimensionally poor in the countries it
    covers, many of them not captured by income lines at all. The <strong>inequality-adjusted
    HDI</strong> discounts the score for how unevenly each dimension is distributed; the
    Gender Inequality Index and Gender Development Index make the field's oldest lesson — that
    development is gendered — visible in the tables. And the income line itself is still used
    carefully: the World Bank's international extreme poverty line (about $2.15 per person per
    day at 2017 PPP) tracks the barest floor, national poverty lines capture what each society
    considers minimally decent, and neither answers the capabilities question alone.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that a rising GDP means the population is becoming better off. GDP measures aggregate market activity; it can rise through extraction, through reconstruction after disasters, through asset bubbles, and through activity that concentrates almost entirely at the top — while median incomes, life expectancy and security stagnate. Development studies treats GDP as one instrument reading among many, not as the patient's pulse.</p>

  <h2>Inequality within and between countries: averages hide people</h2>
  <p>
    National means conceal distributions, and the field's core tool for seeing inside them is
    the <strong>Lorenz curve</strong> (cumulative income share against cumulative population)
    summarised in the <strong>Gini coefficient</strong>: 0 means perfect equality, 100 means
    one person holds everything. The empirical spread is enormous — Nordic countries around
    the high 20s, the United States near 40, and the most unequal large countries (South
    Africa, Brazil) in the 50s and beyond — and the levels matter because they interact: the
    same growth reduces poverty much more where inequality is lower, and high inequality
    converts growth into concentrated gains. The <strong>within/between decomposition</strong>
    is the modern headline: for most of the nineteenth and twentieth centuries, global
    inequality was dominated by <em>between-country</em> gaps (the industrial revolution's
    great divergence); since the 1990s, the rise of Asia has narrowed between-country gaps
    while within-country inequality has widened in much of the rich world — Branko Milanovic's
    famous "elephant curve" traced both movements at once, showing the global middle class
    rising steeply even as rich-country lower middles stagnated.
  </p>
  <p>
    Disaggregation is the methodological discipline that follows. Poverty rates by gender, by
    region, by ethnicity and by age routinely reveal that a country "on track" for its
    aggregate goal contains populations decades behind it — rural girls versus urban boys is
    the classic, persistent slice. Two identical-GDP countries can differ wildly in who is
    poor, and the difference is precisely the development question: which institutions convert
    national income into widespread capability, and which convert it into enclaves. This is
    also why Sen's analysis of famines lands where it does: famine deaths track <em>entitlements</em>
    — who can command food — not national food supplies; democracies with a free press, his
    evidence showed, had not suffered major famines, because public accountability converts
    information into response. Distribution is not a refinement of development; under the
    capabilities definition, it is the substance of it.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    One further worked example shows why the measurement toolkit's details — especially PPP —
    are not technical trivia. Compare a nurse earning the local equivalent of $6,000 a year
    in a low-income country with a nurse earning $30,000 in a rich one. Converted at market
    exchange rates, the gap is fivefold; converted at purchasing power parity, it may be
    closer to twofold — because haircuts, staples, housing and bus fares cost a fraction of
    rich-world prices. The PPP adjustment re-weights both nurses' real consumption and,
    multiplied across economies, moves hundreds of millions of people across the "poverty"
    threshold on paper: the World Bank's headline counts are PPP counts, and each periodic
    PPP benchmark revision (the International Comparison Program's price surveys) reshuffles
    the world's poor without a single income changing. Development statistics therefore
    carry a health warning the field teaches explicitly: the <em>trends</em> are robust, the
    <em>levels</em> are conventions, and any argument that turns on whether a population is
    "just above" or "just below" a line is usually an argument about the line.
  </p>
  <p>
    Three deeper debates carry the foundation into current research. <strong>The politics of
    measurement</strong>: indicators discipline budgets and elections (what gets measured gets
    managed), so indicator choice is itself power — GDP's blind spots, the HDI's compression of
    freedom out of the bundle (Sen's own caveat), the SDGs' 169 targets as a negotiation
    artefact — and the post-2015 stirrings of "beyond GDP" accounting (natural capital, wellbeing
    frameworks, Bhutan's Gross National Happiness as the famous outlier) are attempts to re-run
    the founding definition fight with better instruments. <strong>The convergence question</strong>:
    why did East Asian economies converge on rich-world living standards in two generations
    while much of Sub-Saharan Africa and Latin America did not — institutions, industrial
    policy, geography, colonial legacies, terms of trade, conflict — remains the field's
    organising puzzle, and honest answers are multicausal and contested. <strong>Growth
    itself</strong>: the climate century has reopened the oldest question — whether
    universal prosperity can be achieved within planetary boundaries — splitting the field
    between green-growth optimists, who bet on decoupling emissions from output, and
    post-growth and degrowth currents, who argue rich economies must redefine prosperity
    below GDP's ceiling. What is not contested is the foundation this page laid: development
    is about the expansion of lives, not ledgers; measurement is argument by other means; and
    every average in this discipline is hiding someone whose development has not started.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Cambridge IGCSE Development Studies 0453 (published syllabus); Sen, <em>Development as Freedom</em>; UNDP Human Development Reports; Preston (1975).</p>
